# Cursor Rules – Backend Architecture

## Core Principles
- Controllers are thin
- Services hold business logic
- No function should exceed 25–30 lines if not then Functions should be small and composable
- One responsibility per function
- No hardcoded strings

## Architecture Rules
1. Use module-based structure
2. Each module must contain:
   - controller
   - service
   - routes
   - dto
   - types (if needed)
3. No cross-module imports except via services

## Controllers
- Must NOT:
  - Access database
  - Contain logic
  - Handle errors directly
- Must:
  - Accept/Read request
  - Call service
  - Call validation
  - Return standardized response

## Services
- Contain all business logic
- One function = one responsibility
- Use helper/util functions
- Functions must be small and composable
- reusablility

## Database
- Sequelize with TypeScript
- No raw queries in controllers
- Models must be thin
- Complex queries should go into repositories
- Migrations & seeders required

## Constants & Enums
- No hardcoded strings
- Messages → constants/messages.constant.ts
- Status codes → constants/status-codes.constant.ts
- Enums only for fixed sets (roles, token types)

## Error Handling
- Use centralized custom error classes
- Throw errors from services
- Global error middleware handles responses

## Responses
- Use centralized response helper
- Unified API response format:
  {
    success: boolean,
    message: string,
    data?: any,
    error?: any,
    statusCode?: int
  }

## Validation
- DTO-based validation
- Middleware-based validation
- No inline validation in controllers
- Validate at request boundary
- Never validate inside services

## Authentication
- JWT-based auth only
- Tokens handled via utility functions
- Middleware handles authorization

## Code Style
- TypeScript strict mode
- Named exports only
- No default exports except app/server
- No magic numbers or strings
- Async/await only
- No nested callbacks
- Meaningful naming
- Avoid duplication
- Do not use Fallbacks or Backward Compatability unless it is necessary
- Confirm before adding Fallbacks or Backward Compatability

## Forbidden
- Fat controllers
- God services
- Inline SQL
- try/catch in controllers
- Hardcoded response messages

## Scalability
- Every module should be plug-and-play
- Easy to add new modules without refactor
- Clear separation of concerns
